## Reached at
  The implementation is the libheif project: the source tree in `/app`, built with
  CMake into `/app/build`. Callers reach three project-owned build artifacts by
  absolute path.

  Two executables, invoked through `subprocess` with absolute paths:
      subprocess.run(["/app/build/examples/heif-convert", <input path>, <output path>], ...)
      subprocess.run(["/app/build/examples/heif-info", <input path>], ...)
  Both are built from the project'"'"'s own example sources and are linked against the
  project'"'"'s own shared library.

  The shared library, loaded for C FFI inside a separate child process:
      lib = ctypes.CDLL("/app/build/libheif/libheif.so")
  Before driving it, the caller takes `os.path.realpath("/app/build/libheif/libheif.so")`
  and requires that resolved path to stay under `/app/build/`, and requires each
  library entry point listed below to resolve to an address that lies inside this
  file'"'"'s own memory mapping. A preexisting system library or an interposed shim
  cannot satisfy these callers; the artifacts must come from this project'"'"'s build.

  The environment provides `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/app/build/libheif`, which is how the
  executables resolve the shared library at runtime. Input HEIC files are supplied
  by the caller from its own asset directory; the same entry points are exercised
  with more than one of those files, and the library entry points are exercised on
  handles obtained from each of several input files within one child process.

## Must provide
  /app/build/examples/heif-convert        executable produced by the project build
  /app/build/examples/heif-info           executable produced by the project build
  /app/build/libheif/libheif.so           shared library produced by the project build

  CLI contracts (as the callers use them):
  heif-convert    usage `[-q quality] <filename> <output>`; callers pass only the
                  two positional path arguments
  heif-info       usage `[options] image.heic`; callers pass only the single
                  positional path argument

  Exported C symbols of libheif.so (extern "C"); the caller binds each signature
  through ctypes before calling:
  heif_context_alloc                          function: struct heif_context* (void);
                                              caller binds restype as an opaque pointer
  heif_context_read_from_file                 function: struct heif_error
                                              (struct heif_context*, const char* filename,
                                              const struct heif_reading_options*);
                                              caller binds argtypes [c_void_p, c_char_p, c_void_p]
  heif_context_get_primary_image_handle       function: struct heif_error
                                              (struct heif_context*, struct heif_image_handle**);
                                              caller binds argtypes [c_void_p, POINTER(c_void_p)]
  heif_image_handle_get_luma_bits_per_pixel   function: int (const struct heif_image_handle*);
                                              caller binds restype c_int, argtypes [c_void_p]
  heif_image_handle_get_chroma_bits_per_pixel function: int (const struct heif_image_handle*);
                                              caller binds restype c_int, argtypes [c_void_p]
  heif_image_handle_get_width                 function: int (const struct heif_image_handle*);
                                              caller binds restype c_int, argtypes [c_void_p]
  heif_image_handle_get_height                function: int (const struct heif_image_handle*);
                                              caller binds restype c_int, argtypes [c_void_p]

  struct heif_error                           the by-value error structure of the C API;
                                              three fields in this order: integer code,
                                              integer subcode, const char* message — the
                                              caller declares it exactly this way in ctypes
                                              and reads its first field

## Called as
  heif-convert:
      ["/app/build/examples/heif-convert", <input>, <output>]
        input   — 1st positional argument; a file path string; callers pass paths of
                  HEIC image files
        output  — 2nd positional argument; a file path string; callers use paths
                  ending in `.png` in one run and ending in `.jpg` in another

  heif-info:
      ["/app/build/examples/heif-info", <input>]
        input   — 1st positional argument; a file path string; callers pass paths of
                  HEIC image files

  Library call sequence, driven per input file through the ctypes binding above:
      ctx = lib.heif_context_alloc()
          no arguments
      lib.heif_context_read_from_file(ctx, path, None)
          1st argument: the context pointer
          2nd argument: the file path, passed as bytes (const char*)
          3rd argument: NULL (callers pass None)
      lib.heif_context_get_primary_image_handle(ctx, byref(handle))
          1st argument: the context pointer
          2nd argument: the address of an opaque image-handle pointer variable
      lib.heif_image_handle_get_luma_bits_per_pixel(handle)
          single argument: an opaque image-handle pointer
      lib.heif_image_handle_get_chroma_bits_per_pixel(handle)
          single argument: an opaque image-handle pointer
      lib.heif_image_handle_get_width(handle)
      lib.heif_image_handle_get_height(handle)
          single argument: an opaque image-handle pointer
  The caller reads the first field of the returned heif_error structure after the
  read and handle calls and branches on it. Both bit-depth entry points are
  exercised on handles obtained from input files in the same child process.

## Setup required
  - The CMake build of the `/app` source tree into `/app/build` must have produced
    the three artifacts at exactly the paths above (the image builds them with
    `cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` followed by `make`); source changes only
    reach the callers once these artifacts are rebuilt in place.
  - `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` includes `/app/build/libheif` (already set in the
    environment) so the executables resolve the project'"'"'s shared library.
  - Build prerequisites already present in the environment: a C++ toolchain, CMake,
    and the libde265, libpng, and libjpeg development packages.
  - The caller'"'"'s HEIC input files are present on disk, and the caller verifies the
    digest of each input file before using it.
  - `/usr/bin/python3` (system Python 3) is the interpreter for the child process
    that drives the library surface.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner drives everything: it launches the executables through
  `subprocess.run` with captured output and a per-invocation timeout, and it
  launches the child process for the library surface. None of this runner logic is
  part of the implementation.

  The library surface is reached through an out-of-process child program, run as
  `/usr/bin/python3 -c <inline script> <arguments>`. That inline script performs
  the ctypes binding, the realpath and memory-mapping checks, and the call
  sequence above, and prints one marker line per step for the runner to parse. It
  exists as a separate process so that the runner itself survives whatever happens
  inside the library. The child program, the runner, the `/usr/bin/python3`
  interpreter, and the caller'"'"'s HEIC fixture files all belong to the calling side;
  the engineer does not rebuild any of them.
