## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = http/service (signals: ['"'"'api-v2-route'"'"', '"'"'auth-token-header'"'"', '"'"'delete-endpoint'"'"', '"'"'image-upload-endpoint'"'"', '"'"'http-multipart-upload'"'"', '"'"'stored-file-served-back'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The target is the Organizr PHP application served by nginx over FastCGI
  (php-fpm) at the container'"'"'s HTTP listener on port 80. The application tree
  is staged by the image build at /var/www/html (document root) and reached by
  callers over HTTP, working directory /var/www/html for the FastCGI process:

      GET  /                              (readiness / web root)
      POST /api/v2/login                  (authenticate, obtain token)
      POST /api/v2/image                   (tab-icon image upload)
      DELETE /api/v2/image/{image}         (delete an uploaded image)
      GET  /data/userTabs/{filename}       (serve a stored upload back)

  nginx routes /api/v2/* to /api/v2/index.php via

      location /api/v2/ { try_files $uri $uri/ /api/v2/index.php?$args; }

  and serves /data/userTabs/ straight from the document-root directory
  /var/www/html/data/userTabs via

      location /data/userTabs/ { try_files $uri =404; }

  so a file written under /var/www/html/data/userTabs/{filename} is reachable
  at GET /data/userTabs/{filename}. The application is project-owned source
  staged into /var/www/html by the image build; nginx and php-fpm are the
  preexisting server/runtime that deliver it.

## Must provide
  POST /api/v2/login            HTTP route; accepts a JSON body, authenticates
                                against the application'"'"'s user store, and on
                                success yields an authentication token the
                                caller places in a Token request header.
  POST /api/v2/image            HTTP route; accepts a multipart/form-data
                                file upload named `file` and writes the
                                accepted upload to /var/www/html/data/userTabs.
  DELETE /api/v2/image/{image}   HTTP route; {image} is a path segment naming
                                a previously stored image; operates against the
                                same /var/www/html/data/userTabs storage.
  POST /api/v2/image response   A JSON envelope of shape
                                {"response": {"result": ..., "message": ...}}
                                so callers can read response.response.result
                                and response.response.message.
  Token header authentication    The route must honour a Token request header
                                carrying the value issued by POST /api/v2/login
                                and treat an absent/invalid token as not
                                authenticated.

## Called as
  POST /api/v2/login
    body     JSON object, Content-Type: application/json; object with keys
             username (string), password (string), remember (string).
    caller reads response JSON for response.response.data.token
             (a string) and then sets header Token = <that string> on the
             session for subsequent requests.

  POST /api/v2/image
    multipart/form-data field named `file`, value a 3-tuple
    (filename, content_bytes, mime_type):
      filename      — string; the upload'"'"'s submitted filename (callers use
                      names ending in .png, .jpg, .gif, .svg, and case
                      variants such as .SVG / .Svg / .sVg)
      content_bytes — bytes; the raw file body
      mime_type     — string; the submitted Content-Type for the part
                      (callers use values such as image/png, image/jpeg,
                      image/gif, image/svg+xml)
    caller reads response JSON for response.response.result
             and response.response.message.

  DELETE /api/v2/image/{image}
    {image}   path segment; a string naming a previously stored image
              (the same filename submitted to POST /api/v2/image)
    caller reads response JSON for response.response.result.

  GET /data/userTabs/{filename}
    {filename} path segment; a string naming a stored file (the same
               filename submitted to POST /api/v2/image)
    no request body.

## Setup required
  Service startup: php-fpm and nginx must be running (started by the
  image entrypoint). The application is auto-initialized on first boot via
  POST /api/v2/wizard, which creates /var/www/html/data/config/config.php
  and the admin user account used by callers; before any caller request the
  readiness probe GET / must answer (HTTP 200 or 302).
  Storage: the directory /var/www/html/data/userTabs must exist and be
  web-server-writable so accepted uploads land there and are served at
  GET /data/userTabs/{filename}; it is created and chmod 777 by the image
  build.
  Authentication: callers must first POST /api/v2/login with the admin
  credentials (env-sourced username/password, default admin/admin) and carry
  the returned Token header on every /api/v2/image request; an
  unauthenticated POST /api/v2/image is not accepted.
  Environment: APP_URL (default http://localhost:80), ADMIN_USER
