## Reached at
  The implementation is the source text of one file, reached by pattern extraction
  rather than by require():
      /app/node_modules/dojox/jq.js
  The file is staged by the container image build (an npm-installed dojox package
  inside the /app application tree; the build checks the path exists). Callers
  resolve it by this absolute path only, never through Node package resolution, so
  the reconstructed code must live at exactly this path, edited in place. Runtime
  is Node at /usr/local/bin/node. One caller sets its working directory to /app,
  the other inherits its own; all resolution above uses absolute paths, so nothing
  depends on the working directory.
  A CommonJS helper module shipped with the calling side reads the file as UTF-8
  text and extracts two constructs with whitespace-tolerant patterns:
      /\$\.extend\s*=\s*function\s*\(/                        required; the load fails
                                                              hard if this is not found
      /function\s+jqMix\s*\(\s*obj\s*,\s*props\s*\)\s*/       attempted whenever present
  Each extraction runs from the pattern match to the balanced closing brace,
  skipping string literals and comments while counting braces. The extracted texts
  are then evaluated, in one shared function scope built by the helper, as:
      var dojo = { isObject: <fn>, isArray: <fn>, isIE: false };
      <extracted jqMix text, when found>
      var $ = {};
      <extracted $.extend text>
      return $;
  and the load fails unless the evaluated `$` carries a function at `extend`.
  Callers then drive that function from `node -e` scripts that require() the
  helper and destructure `{ $ }` from it.

## Must provide
  /app/node_modules/dojox/jq.js   readable UTF-8 source file at exactly this path
  $.extend                        an assignment in that source text whose header is
                                  the literal token `$.extend` followed by optional
                                  whitespace, `=`, optional whitespace, `function`,
                                  optional whitespace, `(`; the body must be
                                  brace-balanced and must evaluate to a function on
                                  `$` in the extraction scope
  jqMix                           a function declaration in the same file whose
                                  header matches `function` + whitespace + `jqMix`
                                  + optional whitespace + `(` + optional whitespace
                                  + `obj` + optional whitespace + `,` + optional
                                  whitespace + `props` + optional whitespace + `)`;
                                  required whenever the extracted $.extend text
                                  refers to that name, because both extracted texts
                                  share one scope and nothing else from the file is
                                  visible in it
  external-binding contract       the extracted texts run with exactly one outer
                                  binding available: `dojo`, carrying `isObject`,
                                  `isArray`, and `isIE`; no module system, no other
                                  globals, and no other part of jq.js is reachable
                                  from the extracted code

## Called as
  $.extend(target, source)        target — 1st positional; a plain object
                                  source — 2nd positional; a plain object whose own
                                           keys hold strings, numbers, booleans,
                                           null, arrays, or nested plain objects
  $.extend(target, src1, src2)    3-positional form; the expression'"'"'s value is
                                  bound and consumed by the caller; the 1st
                                  positional is a plain object, each further
                                  positional is a source object as above
  Sources are ordinary objects with own enumerable keys; callers also build a
  merge source by parsing JSON text, so a source may carry any own keys the parsed
  text contains. Both forms are invoked repeatedly in sequence against fresh
  target objects.

## Setup required
  - Node 18 runtime present at /usr/local/bin/node; callers pass the whole program
    as a `-e` argument to it.
  - The extraction scope supplies `dojo.isObject` and `dojo.isArray` with dojo 1.16
    semantics and `dojo.isIE` as the constant false; the extracted code may rely on
    exactly those three bindings. The real dojo package installed alongside the
    target is never loaded for these calls.
  - Brace-matching extraction tolerates reformatting (arbitrary whitespace between
    header tokens) but not an unbalanced body or a header the patterns above do not
    match; a header mismatch means the construct is not extracted at all.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner spawns `/usr/local/bin/node -e "<script>"`; the script requires a
  CommonJS helper that reads the target file by absolute path, extracts the two
  constructs above by regex and brace matching, and evaluates them in a
  `new Function` sandbox with a mocked `dojo` object. The runner, the helper, the
  extraction logic, and the dojo mock all belong to the calling side — they are not
  part of the implementation and must not be rebuilt. Nothing else from the dojox
  file or package is exercised: only the extracted text runs, and only through the
  sandboxed `$`. Any change therefore has to happen inside
  /app/node_modules/dojox/jq.js, in the two constructs named above, so that the
  extraction patterns still match.
```

