This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The target is the application'"'"'s own executable, driven by absolute path with
  no PATH lookup or fallback. Every caller defines
      VIM_BIN = '"'"'/app/src/vim'"'"'
  and spawns it through `subprocess.run([VIM_BIN, ...], capture_output=True,
  text=True, timeout=...)`. The binary is the artifact built from the editor'"'"'s
  C source tree checked out at /app (staged by the image build at a pinned
  upstream tag); the build runs `make` with /app/src as its working directory,
  which produces /app/src/vim. Callers execute that exact path directly — no
  service, no interpreter, no wrapper — and depend on it being the build of
  this tree.

## Must provide
  /app/src/vim    executable ELF binary; callers check `os.path.isfile`,
                  `os.access(..., os.X_OK)`, and the leading b'"'"'\x7fELF'"'"' magic
                  bytes, and do not accept a wrapper script in its place
  --version       CLI flag
  -u {arg}        CLI option; callers pass the string NONE
  -i {arg}        CLI option; callers pass the string NONE
  -n              CLI flag
  -e              CLI flag (Ex mode)
  -s              CLI flag (silent/batch mode, combined with -e)
  -c {command}    CLI option, repeatable; each value is one Ex command string
  -S {path}       CLI option; path of a Vim script file sourced at startup
  trailing operand   optional file path opened as a buffer
  Ex-command surface invoked from the `-c` strings and `-S` scripts:
  `:s` / `:%s` substitution whose replacement text may contain `~` (or `\~`
  when '"'"'nomagic'"'"' is set), `:normal` used to repeat the last substitution with
  `&`, `:set` (option assignments such as nocompatible, nomagic,
  undolevels), `:enew!`, `:put`, line-delete commands, `:w` / `:wq` / `:qa!`
  / `quit`, and `:call` with the script built-ins `setline()`, `getline()`,
  `strlen()`, `writefile()`.

## Called as
  Startup and mode probes:
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'--version'"'"']
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'-u'"'"', '"'"'NONE'"'"', '"'"'-i'"'"', '"'"'NONE'"'"', '"'"'-c'"'"', '"'"'quit'"'"']
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'-e'"'"', '"'"'-s'"'"', '"'"'-c'"'"', '"'"'quit'"'"']
  Editing a file with commands passed as repeated `-c` strings:
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'-e'"'"', '"'"'-s'"'"', '"'"'-c'"'"', '"'"'<ex-command>'"'"', '"'"'-c'"'"', '"'"'wq'"'"', '"'"'<path>'"'"']
          <ex-command>  1st command slot; a string holding one Ex command —
                        callers pass substitution commands of the shape
                        `%s/<pattern>/<replacement>/g`
          <path>        trailing operand; a string path of a caller-created
                        temporary text file
  The same binary driven by a caller-written script file:
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'-e'"'"', '"'"'-s'"'"', '"'"'-S'"'"', '"'"'<script-path>'"'"', '"'"'<path>'"'"']
      [VIM_BIN, '"'"'-u'"'"', '"'"'NONE'"'"', '"'"'-i'"'"', '"'"'NONE'"'"', '"'"'-n'"'"', '"'"'-e'"'"', '"'"'-s'"'"', '"'"'-S'"'"', '"'"'<script-path>'"'"']
          <script-path>  a string path of a temporary `.vim` file written by
                         the caller; its lines are Ex commands — substitution
                         commands whose replacement contains `~` (or `\~` with
                         '"'"'nomagic'"'"' set), `:normal` lines repeating the last
                         substitution with `&`, buffer and file commands, and
                         `:call` lines that pass path strings embedded by the
                         caller as the targets of `writefile()` and `:w`
          <path>         trailing operand; a string path of a caller-created
                         temporary text file (absent in the second form, where
                         the script starts from an empty buffer)

## Setup required
  /app/src/vim must exist as the ELF binary produced by building the source
  tree at /app: `make` is run with /app/src as the working directory, and the
  resulting executable must remain at that exact path. Because callers pin
  ownership by checking the ELF magic and executability, the artifact at
  /app/src/vim must be the build of this tree — not a preinstalled editor from
  PATH and not a script standing in for it. Invocations pass `-u NONE` and
  `-i NONE`, so no vimrc or viminfo is consulted and nothing needs to be
  preconfigured. Callers set no environment variables of their own and do not
  depend on the working directory: all script and fixture paths are absolute
  temporary paths.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest run that spawns the binary with `subprocess.run`
  and captures its streams. It writes the temporary `.vim` script files and
  temporary text fixture files itself (embedding generated path strings in the
  scripts), reads back files written during the run, and deletes the
  temporaries afterwards. Those scripts, fixture files, timeouts, and the
  subprocess plumbing belong to the calling side, not to the implementation;
  none of them are rebuilt as part of the implementation.
