## Called as
  opj_compress CLI (subprocess, absolute paths, per-invocation timeout):
      opj_compress -i <input_path> -o <output_path>
      opj_compress -i <input_path> -o <output_path> -n 1
      opj_compress -h
      -i   input path option; a string; the extension of this path selects
           the decoder, and the TGA inputs carry the .tga extension
      -o   output path option; a string
      -n   optional option; a single decimal-string token
      -h   no argument
  opj_decompress CLI (subprocess, absolute paths):
      opj_decompress -i <codestream_path> -o <output_path>
      both options take path strings
  Direct call, inside the caller-side consumer process (the input file path is
  the consumer'"'"'s argv[1]):
      opj_cparameters_t parameters;
      opj_set_default_encoder_parameters(&parameters);
      opj_image_t *image = tgatoimage(argv[1], &parameters);
      filename    1st argument; a C string holding a file path
      parameters  pointer to an opj_cparameters_t populated with
                  opj_set_default_encoder_parameters before the call

## Setup required
  - Container runtime is Ubuntu 20.04 with build-essential and gcc available;
    the working directory is /app, but every caller invocation uses absolute
    paths only.
  - The image build stages the source tree at /app/openjpeg-2.2.0, builds it
    (cmake Release with BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON and BUILD_CODEC=ON), installs
    into /usr/local and runs ldconfig; LD_LIBRARY_PATH includes
    /usr/local/lib.
  - The installed binaries and the source tree must remain one artifact: the
    CLI route reaches the installed opj_compress, while the direct route
    compiles src/bin/jp2/convert.c straight from the tree on every run. Both
    routes must land on the same reconstructed loader, so neither route may be
    served by a stale install, a wrapper, or a system tool standing in for the
    project'"'"'s own code.
  - Link contract of the direct route: the consumer is built as
        gcc [-static] -O2 -o <out> <consumer.c> \
            /app/openjpeg-2.2.0/src/bin/jp2/convert.c \
            -I<build-tree dirs holding the opj config headers> \
            -I/app/openjpeg-2.2.0/src/bin/jp2 \
            -I/usr/local/include/openjpeg-2.2 \
            -I/app/openjpeg-2.2.0/src/lib/openjp2 \
            <libopenjp2.a or libopenjp2.so> -lpthread -lm
    (-static only when a static archive exists under the build tree), so
    convert.c must keep compiling as a translation unit under those include
    directories and linking against libopenjp2.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side owns: the pytest runner; the subprocess drivers; the TGA
  input byte streams it generates itself and writes to per-run temporary
  directories (an 18-byte TGA header followed by pixel bytes, with width,
  height and pixel-depth fields varying between inputs, and some inputs
  carrying further trailing bytes); the parsers it runs over produced files (a
  J2K codestream header parser and a PPM parser); and the direct-call
  consumer'"'"'s embedded C main together with the per-run gcc compilation that
  produces it. None of these are part of the implementation; the engineer does
  not rebuild them. What the implementation side must supply them is the
  installed opj_compress and opj_decompress binaries, the compilable
  convert.c/convert.h pair exposing tgatoimage, and the libopenjp2 link target
  named above.
